1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,492
There is only one thing in the entire

2
00:00:02,492 --> 00:00:04,783
universe you can be absolutely sure

3
00:00:04,783 --> 00:00:05,356
exists.

4
00:00:06,006 --> 00:00:07,406
Not the chair you're sitting on.

5
00:00:08,056 --> 00:00:09,370
Not the people you love.

6
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:11,823
Not even the screen in front of you.

7
00:00:12,473 --> 00:00:14,090
Just one thing: your own mind.

8
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:17,384
Everything else — every face, every voice,

9
00:00:17,384 --> 00:00:19,911
every memory of a world outside — could,

10
00:00:19,911 --> 00:00:22,379
in principle, be something your mind is

11
00:00:22,379 --> 00:00:23,789
producing on its own.

12
00:00:24,439 --> 00:00:26,333
This idea is called solipsism.

13
00:00:26,983 --> 00:00:29,111
And here's what makes it unsettling:

14
00:00:29,111 --> 00:00:31,512
for more than two thousand years, some of

15
00:00:31,512 --> 00:00:33,695
the greatest thinkers in history have

16
00:00:33,695 --> 00:00:35,278
tried to prove it wrong...

17
00:00:35,928 --> 00:00:37,910
...and they still argue about whether

18
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:39,100
anyone has succeeded.

19
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:41,730
By the end of this video, you'll know

20
00:00:41,730 --> 00:00:43,165
where this idea came from,

21
00:00:43,815 --> 00:00:45,738
why your brain spent years of your

22
00:00:45,738 --> 00:00:47,557
childhood learning to escape it,

23
00:00:48,207 --> 00:00:50,298
and why one famous logician openly

24
00:00:50,298 --> 00:00:51,994
admitted she believed it.

25
00:00:52,644 --> 00:00:54,506
Quick favor before we start:

26
00:00:54,506 --> 00:00:57,149
if you like ideas that break your brain a

27
00:00:57,149 --> 00:00:58,531
little, subscribe.

28
00:00:58,531 --> 00:01:00,573
It genuinely helps the channel.

29
00:01:01,223 --> 00:01:02,874
The word comes from Latin:

30
00:01:02,874 --> 00:01:05,152
solus, meaning "alone," and ipse,

31
00:01:05,152 --> 00:01:07,372
meaning "self." Alone with the self.

32
00:01:08,022 --> 00:01:09,884
Philosophers usually separate two

33
00:01:09,884 --> 00:01:10,505
versions.

34
00:01:11,155 --> 00:01:13,533
The strong version, metaphysical

35
00:01:13,533 --> 00:01:14,855
solipsism, says:

36
00:01:14,855 --> 00:01:16,375
only my mind exists.

37
00:01:16,375 --> 00:01:19,282
That's it. The world and other people are

38
00:01:19,282 --> 00:01:21,528
just contents of my experience.

39
00:01:22,178 --> 00:01:24,548
The weaker version, epistemological

40
00:01:24,548 --> 00:01:26,978
solipsism, is more modest — and much

41
00:01:26,978 --> 00:01:28,193
harder to escape.

42
00:01:28,843 --> 00:01:31,401
It says: I can't know that anything

43
00:01:31,401 --> 00:01:33,064
outside my mind exists.

44
00:01:33,064 --> 00:01:35,623
Maybe it does. I just can't prove it.

45
00:01:36,273 --> 00:01:38,390
Most philosophers reject the first.

46
00:01:38,390 --> 00:01:40,620
The second is the one that keeps them

47
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:41,623
awake at night.

48
00:01:42,273 --> 00:01:44,380
The idea is older than you might think.

49
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:47,547
Around the fifth century BC, the Greek

50
00:01:47,547 --> 00:01:50,064
thinker Gorgias wrote a work usually

51
00:01:50,064 --> 00:01:51,599
called On Non-Being.

52
00:01:52,249 --> 00:01:54,353
Its argument came in three steps:

53
00:01:54,353 --> 00:01:56,983
nothing exists; even if something existed,

54
00:01:56,983 --> 00:01:59,613
it couldn't be known; and even if it could

55
00:01:59,613 --> 00:02:02,126
be known, it couldn't be communicated to

56
00:02:02,126 --> 00:02:03,003
anyone else.

57
00:02:03,653 --> 00:02:06,361
Whether Gorgias meant it seriously or as a

58
00:02:06,361 --> 00:02:08,889
showpiece of clever rhetoric, the third

59
00:02:08,889 --> 00:02:11,237
step points straight at our problem:

60
00:02:11,237 --> 00:02:13,765
your experience is locked inside you.

61
00:02:14,415 --> 00:02:16,483
In ancient China, the philosopher

62
00:02:16,483 --> 00:02:18,437
Zhuangzi told a famous story.

63
00:02:19,087 --> 00:02:21,835
He dreamed he was a butterfly, fluttering

64
00:02:21,835 --> 00:02:24,396
happily, with no idea he was Zhuangzi.

65
00:02:25,046 --> 00:02:27,502
Then he woke up — and wasn't sure anymore.

66
00:02:28,152 --> 00:02:30,636
Was he a man who had dreamed of being a

67
00:02:30,636 --> 00:02:33,180
butterfly... or a butterfly now dreaming

68
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:34,126
he was a man?

69
00:02:34,776 --> 00:02:37,122
Meanwhile, in India, a similar question

70
00:02:37,122 --> 00:02:38,518
took a different path.

71
00:02:39,168 --> 00:02:41,601
Around the fourth or fifth century, the

72
00:02:41,601 --> 00:02:44,150
Buddhist philosopher Vasubandhu wrote a

73
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:46,641
short text known as the Twenty Verses.

74
00:02:47,291 --> 00:02:49,606
His school, Yogācāra, is often called

75
00:02:49,606 --> 00:02:50,928
"consciousness-only."

76
00:02:51,578 --> 00:02:53,847
His starting point sounds familiar:

77
00:02:53,847 --> 00:02:56,355
in a dream, you see places, people, and

78
00:02:56,355 --> 00:02:58,863
objects that feel completely real — yet

79
00:02:58,863 --> 00:03:01,013
there's nothing outside your mind

80
00:03:01,013 --> 00:03:02,088
producing them.

81
00:03:02,738 --> 00:03:04,557
So how do we know waking life is

82
00:03:04,557 --> 00:03:05,232
different?

83
00:03:05,882 --> 00:03:07,318
But here's the twist:

84
00:03:07,318 --> 00:03:09,471
Vasubandhu was not a solipsist.

85
00:03:10,121 --> 00:03:12,559
His tradition accepted many minds — many

86
00:03:12,559 --> 00:03:15,053
streams of consciousness, influencing one

87
00:03:15,053 --> 00:03:15,677
another.

88
00:03:16,327 --> 00:03:19,074
The question wasn't "Am I the only one?"

89
00:03:19,074 --> 00:03:21,757
It was "What are we really experiencing

90
00:03:21,757 --> 00:03:23,738
when we experience a world?"

91
00:03:24,388 --> 00:03:26,738
Centuries later, the Hindu philosopher

92
00:03:26,738 --> 00:03:29,145
Adi Shankara taught that the everyday

93
00:03:29,145 --> 00:03:31,380
world of separate things is maya —

94
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:32,239
appearance —

95
00:03:32,889 --> 00:03:35,101
and that, at the deepest level, there is

96
00:03:35,101 --> 00:03:35,975
only one Self.

97
00:03:36,625 --> 00:03:39,046
That's not "only my mind exists" either.

98
00:03:39,046 --> 00:03:41,524
It's closer to: the line between "me" and

99
00:03:41,524 --> 00:03:43,776
"everything else" may not be where we

100
00:03:43,776 --> 00:03:44,283
think.

101
00:03:44,933 --> 00:03:48,094
Two thousand years later, in the 1600s, a

102
00:03:48,094 --> 00:03:51,184
French philosopher decided to take doubt

103
00:03:51,184 --> 00:03:52,980
to its absolute limit.

104
00:03:53,630 --> 00:03:55,461
His name was René Descartes.

105
00:03:56,111 --> 00:03:58,242
His plan was simple and radical:

106
00:03:58,242 --> 00:04:00,982
throw out every belief that could possibly

107
00:04:00,982 --> 00:04:03,539
be false, and see if anything survives.

108
00:04:04,189 --> 00:04:05,379
First, the senses.

109
00:04:05,379 --> 00:04:07,702
They fool us all the time — a straight

110
00:04:07,702 --> 00:04:09,345
stick looks bent in water.

111
00:04:09,995 --> 00:04:11,612
So they can't be fully trusted.

112
00:04:12,262 --> 00:04:13,904
Then, the dream argument.

113
00:04:13,904 --> 00:04:16,203
Every night you experience vivid worlds

114
00:04:16,203 --> 00:04:18,612
that feel completely real while you're in

115
00:04:18,612 --> 00:04:19,050
them.

116
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:21,629
Can you be certain, right now, that you're

117
00:04:21,629 --> 00:04:22,014
awake?

118
00:04:22,664 --> 00:04:24,625
And finally, his most famous thought

119
00:04:24,625 --> 00:04:25,329
experiment.

120
00:04:25,979 --> 00:04:28,316
What if there were an evil demon,

121
00:04:28,316 --> 00:04:31,022
supremely powerful and cunning, using all

122
00:04:31,022 --> 00:04:32,868
its energy to deceive you —

123
00:04:33,518 --> 00:04:35,883
feeding you a fake world, fake people,

124
00:04:35,883 --> 00:04:37,326
even fake mathematics?

125
00:04:37,976 --> 00:04:40,308
Descartes pushed until almost nothing was

126
00:04:40,308 --> 00:04:40,732
left.

127
00:04:41,382 --> 00:04:43,522
And then he found the one thing the demon

128
00:04:43,522 --> 00:04:44,494
could never fake.

129
00:04:45,144 --> 00:04:47,344
If you are being deceived, then you exist

130
00:04:47,344 --> 00:04:49,444
— someone must be there to be deceived.

131
00:04:50,094 --> 00:04:52,064
Doubting is a kind of thinking, and

132
00:04:52,064 --> 00:04:53,672
thinking requires a thinker.

133
00:04:54,322 --> 00:04:56,411
In his words: I think, therefore I am.

134
00:04:57,061 --> 00:04:59,259
It's one of the most famous sentences in

135
00:04:59,259 --> 00:04:59,974
philosophy.

136
00:05:00,624 --> 00:05:02,717
But notice what it actually proves.

137
00:05:02,717 --> 00:05:04,369
It proves that you exist.

138
00:05:05,019 --> 00:05:07,356
Not your body — just your thinking.

139
00:05:08,006 --> 00:05:10,165
And it says absolutely nothing about

140
00:05:10,165 --> 00:05:10,995
anyone else.

141
00:05:11,645 --> 00:05:13,903
Descartes had dug himself into a hole, and

142
00:05:13,903 --> 00:05:14,605
he knew it.

143
00:05:15,255 --> 00:05:17,867
To climb out, he argued that a perfect God

144
00:05:17,867 --> 00:05:20,073
exists and would not allow us to be

145
00:05:20,073 --> 00:05:21,756
deceived about everything.

146
00:05:22,406 --> 00:05:24,642
Many later readers found that step much

147
00:05:24,642 --> 00:05:27,037
weaker than the doubt that came before it.

148
00:05:27,687 --> 00:05:29,569
The hole he dug was convincing.

149
00:05:29,569 --> 00:05:31,118
The ladder out was not.

150
00:05:31,768 --> 00:05:34,241
A century later, the Irish bishop George

151
00:05:34,241 --> 00:05:35,983
Berkeley went even further.

152
00:05:36,633 --> 00:05:39,933
In 1710 he argued that physical objects

153
00:05:39,933 --> 00:05:42,998
are nothing more than collections of

154
00:05:42,998 --> 00:05:44,177
perceptions.

155
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:46,628
To be, he said, is to be perceived.

156
00:05:47,278 --> 00:05:49,710
Berkeley wasn't a solipsist — he believed

157
00:05:49,710 --> 00:05:51,533
other minds and God were real.

158
00:05:52,183 --> 00:05:54,123
But critics said his ideas slid

159
00:05:54,123 --> 00:05:55,322
dangerously close.

160
00:05:55,972 --> 00:05:58,157
His most famous critic didn't use an

161
00:05:58,157 --> 00:05:59,222
argument at all.

162
00:05:59,872 --> 00:06:03,144
In 1763, the writer Samuel Johnson was

163
00:06:03,144 --> 00:06:06,568
walking with his biographer James Boswell,

164
00:06:06,568 --> 00:06:09,764
who remarked that Berkeley's theory was

165
00:06:09,764 --> 00:06:11,591
impossible to refute.

166
00:06:12,241 --> 00:06:14,959
Johnson walked up to a large stone, kicked

167
00:06:14,959 --> 00:06:17,557
it hard enough to bounce back, and said:

168
00:06:17,557 --> 00:06:19,007
"I refute it thus."

169
00:06:19,657 --> 00:06:21,087
It's a great moment.

170
00:06:21,087 --> 00:06:23,884
It's also, philosophers will tell you, not

171
00:06:23,884 --> 00:06:25,314
a refutation at all.

172
00:06:25,964 --> 00:06:28,228
Berkeley never denied that you feel the

173
00:06:28,228 --> 00:06:30,543
stone. He only questioned what the feeling

174
00:06:30,543 --> 00:06:31,264
is made of.

175
00:06:31,914 --> 00:06:34,113
And this brings us to the heart of it — a

176
00:06:34,113 --> 00:06:36,262
puzzle philosophers call the problem of

177
00:06:36,262 --> 00:06:37,062
other minds.

178
00:06:37,712 --> 00:06:39,723
You see other people laugh, cry, flinch

179
00:06:39,723 --> 00:06:40,729
when they're hurt.

180
00:06:41,379 --> 00:06:43,623
But you never experience their pain.

181
00:06:43,623 --> 00:06:45,234
You only see the outside:

182
00:06:45,234 --> 00:06:47,017
a face, a sound, a movement.

183
00:06:47,667 --> 00:06:49,800
Their inner world, if there is one, is

184
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:51,413
permanently hidden from you.

185
00:06:52,063 --> 00:06:55,548
In 1865, John Stuart Mill offered the

186
00:06:55,548 --> 00:06:58,619
classic answer: the argument from

187
00:06:58,619 --> 00:06:59,614
analogy.

188
00:07:00,264 --> 00:07:01,866
When I'm hurt, I wince.

189
00:07:01,866 --> 00:07:04,268
Others wince in the same situations.

190
00:07:04,268 --> 00:07:06,978
So, by analogy, they probably feel what I

191
00:07:06,978 --> 00:07:07,470
feel.

192
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:10,628
It sounds reasonable — until you notice

193
00:07:10,628 --> 00:07:13,015
the problem. You're generalizing from

194
00:07:13,015 --> 00:07:14,508
exactly one example:

195
00:07:14,508 --> 00:07:15,224
yourself.

196
00:07:15,874 --> 00:07:18,202
Modern philosophers have made the puzzle

197
00:07:18,202 --> 00:07:19,068
even sharper.

198
00:07:19,718 --> 00:07:23,174
In 1996, David Chalmers popularized the

199
00:07:23,174 --> 00:07:26,001
idea of a philosophical zombie:

200
00:07:26,651 --> 00:07:29,446
a being that looks and acts exactly like a

201
00:07:29,446 --> 00:07:32,179
person, says "ouch," writes poetry, falls

202
00:07:32,179 --> 00:07:32,924
in love —

203
00:07:33,574 --> 00:07:35,524
but has no inner experience at all.

204
00:07:36,174 --> 00:07:38,149
The lights are on, but nobody's home.

205
00:07:38,799 --> 00:07:40,717
Here's the uncomfortable question:

206
00:07:40,717 --> 00:07:43,050
from the outside, how would you ever tell

207
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:44,036
the difference?

208
00:07:44,686 --> 00:07:45,936
Let's try something.

209
00:07:45,936 --> 00:07:48,382
Think of someone you know well — a friend,

210
00:07:48,382 --> 00:07:49,034
a parent.

211
00:07:49,684 --> 00:07:51,977
Now try to prove — not believe, but

212
00:07:51,977 --> 00:07:54,385
prove — that they feel anything at all.

213
00:07:55,035 --> 00:07:57,874
You'll notice that every piece of evidence

214
00:07:57,874 --> 00:08:00,397
is behavior: words, faces, reactions.

215
00:08:01,047 --> 00:08:03,267
Now flip it. How would they prove that

216
00:08:03,267 --> 00:08:04,454
you feel anything?

217
00:08:05,104 --> 00:08:07,628
You are certain about your own pain, and

218
00:08:07,628 --> 00:08:09,683
uncertain about everyone else's.

219
00:08:09,683 --> 00:08:12,207
That asymmetry is the whole problem in a

220
00:08:12,207 --> 00:08:13,205
single breath.

221
00:08:13,855 --> 00:08:16,102
There's even a word for the opposite

222
00:08:16,102 --> 00:08:18,521
feeling: sonder, coined by the writer

223
00:08:18,521 --> 00:08:19,385
John Koenig.

224
00:08:20,035 --> 00:08:22,515
It's the sudden realization that every

225
00:08:22,515 --> 00:08:25,055
stranger around you has a life as vivid

226
00:08:25,055 --> 00:08:26,929
and complicated as your own.

227
00:08:27,579 --> 00:08:29,972
And here's where psychology gives us a

228
00:08:29,972 --> 00:08:31,139
surprising twist.

229
00:08:31,139 --> 00:08:33,765
In a sense, every one of us starts life as

230
00:08:33,765 --> 00:08:35,107
a kind of solipsist.

231
00:08:35,757 --> 00:08:38,118
Very young children struggle to understand

232
00:08:38,118 --> 00:08:40,373
that other people have beliefs different

233
00:08:40,373 --> 00:08:41,317
from their own.

234
00:08:41,967 --> 00:08:44,343
Psychologists test this with what's called

235
00:08:44,343 --> 00:08:45,663
a false-belief task.

236
00:08:46,313 --> 00:08:49,185
In one well-known version, the Sally–Anne

237
00:08:49,185 --> 00:08:51,993
test, a child watches a doll named Sally

238
00:08:51,993 --> 00:08:54,417
put a marble in a basket and leave.

239
00:08:55,067 --> 00:08:57,326
While she's gone, Anne moves the marble

240
00:08:57,326 --> 00:08:58,078
into a box.

241
00:08:58,728 --> 00:09:00,057
Then the child is asked:

242
00:09:00,057 --> 00:09:02,025
when Sally comes back, where will she

243
00:09:02,025 --> 00:09:02,419
look?

244
00:09:03,069 --> 00:09:04,429
Most three-year-olds say:

245
00:09:04,429 --> 00:09:06,615
the box — because that's where they know

246
00:09:06,615 --> 00:09:07,052
it is.

247
00:09:07,702 --> 00:09:10,109
Around age four, most children start to

248
00:09:10,109 --> 00:09:11,370
answer: the basket.

249
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:14,098
They've realized something huge:

250
00:09:14,098 --> 00:09:16,769
other people have minds of their own, with

251
00:09:16,769 --> 00:09:19,440
their own, sometimes wrong, picture of the

252
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:19,974
world.

253
00:09:20,624 --> 00:09:23,091
Psychologists call this ability theory of

254
00:09:23,091 --> 00:09:23,584
mind.

255
00:09:24,234 --> 00:09:26,695
So in a way, your brain already made its

256
00:09:26,695 --> 00:09:29,213
leap out of solipsism — not by proof, but

257
00:09:29,213 --> 00:09:30,358
by development.

258
00:09:31,008 --> 00:09:33,235
And sometimes, that sense of reality can

259
00:09:33,235 --> 00:09:33,649
slip.

260
00:09:34,299 --> 00:09:36,717
Many people experience brief moments of

261
00:09:36,717 --> 00:09:39,307
derealization — a feeling that the world

262
00:09:39,307 --> 00:09:41,668
is flat, distant, dreamlike, not quite

263
00:09:41,668 --> 00:09:42,128
real.

264
00:09:42,778 --> 00:09:45,544
When it's persistent and distressing, it's

265
00:09:45,544 --> 00:09:47,879
recognized as a clinical condition,

266
00:09:47,879 --> 00:09:50,031
depersonalization-derealization

267
00:09:50,031 --> 00:09:52,735
disorder, estimated to affect around two

268
00:09:52,735 --> 00:09:54,026
percent of people.

269
00:09:54,676 --> 00:09:57,570
In the 2000s, psychiatrist Joel Gold and

270
00:09:57,570 --> 00:10:00,336
his brother, the philosopher Ian Gold,

271
00:10:00,336 --> 00:10:02,974
began describing patients who believed

272
00:10:02,974 --> 00:10:05,611
their entire life was a staged reality

273
00:10:05,611 --> 00:10:08,506
show, with everyone around them as actors.

274
00:10:09,156 --> 00:10:11,214
They called it the Truman Show delusion.

275
00:10:11,864 --> 00:10:14,251
The philosophers' thought experiment, it

276
00:10:14,251 --> 00:10:16,472
turns out, can become someone's lived

277
00:10:16,472 --> 00:10:17,249
experience.

278
00:10:17,899 --> 00:10:19,699
Now, the logician I promised you.

279
00:10:20,349 --> 00:10:23,036
Christine Ladd-Franklin was a brilliant

280
00:10:23,036 --> 00:10:25,356
American logician and psychologist.

281
00:10:26,006 --> 00:10:28,639
In his 1948 book Human Knowledge,

282
00:10:28,639 --> 00:10:31,548
Bertrand Russell recalled receiving a

283
00:10:31,548 --> 00:10:32,865
letter from her.

284
00:10:33,515 --> 00:10:35,460
She told him she was a solipsist — and

285
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:37,357
that she was surprised there were no

286
00:10:37,357 --> 00:10:37,879
others.

287
00:10:38,529 --> 00:10:40,897
Think about that sentence for a second.

288
00:10:40,897 --> 00:10:43,378
If solipsism is true, of course there are

289
00:10:43,378 --> 00:10:45,465
no others. That's the whole point.

290
00:10:46,115 --> 00:10:47,516
And yet she was surprised.

291
00:10:48,166 --> 00:10:50,991
It's funny. But it also shows something

292
00:10:50,991 --> 00:10:53,951
important: solipsism is almost impossible

293
00:10:53,951 --> 00:10:55,700
to live consistently.

294
00:10:56,350 --> 00:10:58,774
The moment you talk, write, argue, or

295
00:10:58,774 --> 00:11:01,320
expect to be understood, you're already

296
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:03,866
acting as if other minds are out there.

297
00:11:04,516 --> 00:11:06,654
The Austrian philosopher Ludwig

298
00:11:06,654 --> 00:11:09,402
Wittgenstein turned that observation into

299
00:11:09,402 --> 00:11:11,845
one of the most powerful responses to

300
00:11:11,845 --> 00:11:12,639
solipsism.

301
00:11:13,289 --> 00:11:15,320
His point, roughly, was this:

302
00:11:15,320 --> 00:11:17,733
the solipsist expresses the idea in

303
00:11:17,733 --> 00:11:20,146
language. But language is public.

304
00:11:20,796 --> 00:11:23,249
Words get their meaning from shared rules,

305
00:11:23,249 --> 00:11:24,612
from use among people.

306
00:11:25,262 --> 00:11:27,792
A truly private language — one that only

307
00:11:27,792 --> 00:11:30,210
you could ever understand — may not even

308
00:11:30,210 --> 00:11:31,054
be possible.

309
00:11:31,704 --> 00:11:34,530
So the very sentence "only my mind exists"

310
00:11:34,530 --> 00:11:37,231
borrows its meaning from a world full of

311
00:11:37,231 --> 00:11:38,361
other speakers.

312
00:11:39,011 --> 00:11:41,673
Strangely, in his early work, Wittgenstein

313
00:11:41,673 --> 00:11:44,334
also wrote that what the solipsist means

314
00:11:44,334 --> 00:11:46,818
is, in a sense, quite correct — it just

315
00:11:46,818 --> 00:11:48,001
cannot be said.

316
00:11:48,651 --> 00:11:50,288
It can only show itself.

317
00:11:50,288 --> 00:11:52,956
Philosophers are still arguing about what

318
00:11:52,956 --> 00:11:54,168
exactly he meant.

319
00:11:54,818 --> 00:11:57,103
Today, the old demon has a new costume.

320
00:11:57,753 --> 00:12:01,024
In 1981, the philosopher Hilary Putnam

321
00:12:01,024 --> 00:12:03,458
described a brain in a vat:

322
00:12:04,108 --> 00:12:07,063
a brain kept alive by scientists, wired to

323
00:12:07,063 --> 00:12:09,624
a computer that feeds it a perfectly

324
00:12:09,624 --> 00:12:10,937
convincing world.

325
00:12:11,587 --> 00:12:13,341
How could you know you're not that brain?

326
00:12:13,991 --> 00:12:16,254
Putnam himself argued the scenario

327
00:12:16,254 --> 00:12:18,761
undermines itself — that if you really

328
00:12:18,761 --> 00:12:21,268
were such a brain, your words couldn't

329
00:12:21,268 --> 00:12:23,592
even refer to real brains and vats.

330
00:12:24,242 --> 00:12:26,328
Not everyone accepts his argument.

331
00:12:26,978 --> 00:12:29,713
And in 2003, Nick Bostrom published the

332
00:12:29,713 --> 00:12:32,262
simulation argument, suggesting that

333
00:12:32,262 --> 00:12:34,811
under certain assumptions, we might be

334
00:12:34,811 --> 00:12:36,988
living in a computer simulation.

335
00:12:37,638 --> 00:12:40,355
That isn't solipsism — a simulation could

336
00:12:40,355 --> 00:12:42,023
contain many real minds.

337
00:12:42,673 --> 00:12:45,082
But it reopens Descartes's question:

338
00:12:45,082 --> 00:12:47,676
how much of what you experience can you

339
00:12:47,676 --> 00:12:48,973
actually verify?

340
00:12:49,623 --> 00:12:51,492
Pop culture loves this question.

341
00:12:52,142 --> 00:12:54,543
In The Truman Show, a man slowly

342
00:12:54,543 --> 00:12:57,462
discovers that his whole life is a TV set,

343
00:12:57,462 --> 00:12:59,863
and everyone he knows is an actor.

344
00:13:00,512 --> 00:13:03,157
In The Matrix, the world is a simulation

345
00:13:03,157 --> 00:13:05,567
fed into people's brains — Descartes's

346
00:13:05,567 --> 00:13:07,330
demon with better graphics.

347
00:13:07,980 --> 00:13:10,249
In Inception, characters carry small

348
00:13:10,249 --> 00:13:12,684
objects to test whether they're dreaming.

349
00:13:13,334 --> 00:13:15,744
None of these stories are really solipsism

350
00:13:15,744 --> 00:13:17,994
— there are always other real people in

351
00:13:17,994 --> 00:13:18,423
them.

352
00:13:19,073 --> 00:13:20,527
Maybe that's telling:

353
00:13:20,527 --> 00:13:22,950
even our wildest fantasies about fake

354
00:13:22,950 --> 00:13:25,615
worlds can't quite let go of other minds.

355
00:13:26,265 --> 00:13:28,357
So why isn't everyone a solipsist?

356
00:13:29,007 --> 00:13:30,959
One answer: inference to the best

357
00:13:30,959 --> 00:13:31,804
explanation.

358
00:13:32,454 --> 00:13:35,037
The world behaves too consistently, too

359
00:13:35,037 --> 00:13:37,620
surprisingly, too independently of your

360
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:38,235
wishes.

361
00:13:38,885 --> 00:13:41,067
Other people tell you things you didn't

362
00:13:41,067 --> 00:13:43,197
know, disagree with you, surprise you.

363
00:13:43,847 --> 00:13:45,988
The simplest explanation is that they're

364
00:13:45,988 --> 00:13:46,486
real.

365
00:13:47,136 --> 00:13:49,485
And another answer comes from the Scottish

366
00:13:49,485 --> 00:13:50,946
philosopher David Hume.

367
00:13:51,596 --> 00:13:54,173
Hume took skeptical doubts seriously — and

368
00:13:54,173 --> 00:13:56,692
admitted they didn't survive contact with

369
00:13:56,692 --> 00:13:57,665
ordinary life.

370
00:13:58,315 --> 00:14:00,636
He wrote that he would dine, play a game

371
00:14:00,636 --> 00:14:02,903
of backgammon, talk and be merry with

372
00:14:02,903 --> 00:14:03,713
his friends,

373
00:14:04,363 --> 00:14:06,822
and after a few hours, his speculations

374
00:14:06,822 --> 00:14:09,282
would seem so cold and strained that he

375
00:14:09,282 --> 00:14:11,858
couldn't bring himself to return to them.

376
00:14:12,508 --> 00:14:14,829
Nature, in other words, is stronger than

377
00:14:14,829 --> 00:14:15,621
philosophy.

378
00:14:16,271 --> 00:14:17,671
So here's where we end up.

379
00:14:18,321 --> 00:14:20,941
You cannot prove, with absolute certainty,

380
00:14:20,941 --> 00:14:22,747
that anyone else has a mind.

381
00:14:23,397 --> 00:14:24,571
Not your friends.

382
00:14:24,571 --> 00:14:27,038
Not your family. Not the person reading

383
00:14:27,038 --> 00:14:28,976
the comments under this video.

384
00:14:29,626 --> 00:14:31,793
But every day, you act as if they do.

385
00:14:32,443 --> 00:14:34,460
You apologize. You explain.

386
00:14:34,460 --> 00:14:36,544
You hope to be understood.

387
00:14:37,194 --> 00:14:40,071
Maybe that's the real answer to solipsism.

388
00:14:40,071 --> 00:14:42,118
Not a proof — but a choice.

389
00:14:42,768 --> 00:14:45,434
A choice to treat the minds around you as

390
00:14:45,434 --> 00:14:47,677
real, because the alternative is a

391
00:14:47,677 --> 00:14:50,040
universe with only one person in it.

392
00:14:50,690 --> 00:14:52,938
Because in the end, there is only one

393
00:14:52,938 --> 00:14:55,466
thing you can be absolutely sure exists...

394
00:14:55,466 --> 00:14:57,826
and it's lonely in there by yourself.

